//! Copper is generated by an external autorouter, never by Synth itself.
//!
//! Synth owns everything up to the copper: source parsing, topology,
//! placement, board/layer/net-class constraints, and the deterministic
//! export boundary. This crate owns everything after it: selecting a
//! routing engine, running it inside hard resource limits, and deciding
//! — independently of the router's own bookkeeping — whether what came
//! back may be called routed.
//!
//! The separation exists because a router's exit code is a claim, not a
//! result. FreeRouting exits `0` after routing nothing at all; KiCadRoutingTools
//! exits `0` while leaving open nets behind. Treating either as success is
//! how an empty board reaches a fabricator. So every candidate passes the
//! same downstream pipeline regardless of which engine produced it:
//!
//! 1. the un-routed export is retained as `<stem>.synth.kicad_pcb` and is
//!    never overwritten by a failed or partial run ([`RouteRequest::baseline`]);
//! 2. the engine runs with a wall-clock budget, an output cap, and an
//!    optional cancellation token ([`process`]);
//! 3. the candidate board is re-read from disk ([`pcb_read`]) rather than
//!    trusted, and checked against the baseline it was routed from;
//! 4. connectivity is re-derived from copper geometry — union-find over
//!    pads, tracks, and vias — never read from the router's own bookkeeping
//!    ([`validate::check_connectivity`]);
//! 5. zones are refilled and `kicad-cli pcb drc` runs against the resolved
//!    fabrication profile;
//! 6. only a candidate that survives all of that becomes [`RouteState::Routed`].
//!
//! Four terminal states stay distinct because they call for different human
//! responses: [`RouteState::Routed`], [`RouteState::ReviewRequired`],
//! [`RouteState::RouterUnavailable`], and [`RouteState::ValidationFailed`].
//! Collapsing any two of them — most dangerously treating an unavailable
//! router as a pass — is the failure mode this crate exists to prevent.
//!
//! Selection is explicit. If the chosen engine is missing, the run fails
//! with [`RouterFailureReason`] naming what to install; it never silently
//! falls back to another engine, and there is no built-in router to fall
//! back to.
//!
//! [`RouteRequest::baseline`]: RouteRequest::baseline_path

